description:
EXPLOITABLE NOW as a denial of service against personad.

persona-attestors/src/ssh.rs:59-64 in agent_roundtrip():
    let mut len_buf = [0u8; 4];
    stream.read_exact(&mut len_buf).await?;
    let resp_len = u32::from_be_bytes(len_buf) as usize;
    let mut resp = vec![0u8; resp_len];
    stream.read_exact(&mut resp).await?;
The length is taken from the peer and used directly to size an allocation with no upper bound. The SSH agent protocol caps messages at 256 KiB; nothing here enforces that.

TRIGGER: personad connects to whatever $SSH_AUTH_SOCK points at (ssh.rs:32-37 and the connect site in enumerate). A hostile or compromised agent - or any process that can set SSH_AUTH_SOCK in the daemons environment, or bind a socket at that path - replies with the four bytes ff ff ff ff. The daemon immediately tries to zero-fill a 4 GiB Vec.

CONSEQUENCE: allocation failure aborts the process (Rust allocation failure is not catchable), or the zero-fill drives the machine into swap/OOM-kill. Either way the identity daemon dies, and since the CA key is ephemeral (persona-core/src/signer.rs:53-54) every previously issued JWT-SVID becomes unverifiable on restart - so a cheap DoS also invalidates outstanding credentials.

Note read_string() (ssh.rs:67-77) does bound-check correctly against the buffer it was handed; the defect is only at the framing read. Fix is one line: reject resp_len > 256 * 1024 before allocating.
